That "shift" or "jump" is a common issue when coordinating pinning with other scroll-based animations. It's happening because two separate `ScrollTrigger` instances are activating at the same time: one is pinning the `.films-section`, and the other is starting the parallax animation on the `.content-section`.

When an element is pinned, its CSS position is changed, which can cause a layout recalculation. If another animation starts at that exact moment, it can lead to a visual jump as the browser tries to reconcile both effects simultaneously.

To fix this, we can use a single GSAP Timeline to manage both the pinning and the animation. This allows `ScrollTrigger` to handle them as one coordinated sequence, ensuring a smooth transition.

Here is how we can adjust the code in `useEffect`:

```javascript
  useEffect(() => {
    gsap.to(".hero-section", {
      yPercent: 20,
      ease: "none",
      scrollTrigger: {
        trigger: ".page-container",
        start: "top top",
        end: "bottom bottom",
        scrub: true,
      },
    });

    const tl = gsap.timeline({
      scrollTrigger: {
        trigger: ".films-section",
        start: "top top",
        pin: true,
        pinSpacing: true,
        scrub: 1.5,
        end: () => `+=${document.querySelector(".films-section")?.offsetHeight || 0}`,
      },
    });

    tl.to(".content-section", {
      yPercent: -40,
      ease: "none",
    });

  }, []);
```

This approach creates a timeline that pins the `.films-section` and applies the parallax effect to the `.content-section` within that same timeline, which should eliminate the shift.

